refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3711:`auroc_tool`, `auroc_direct`, and `delta` use macro AUROC when available, but the bootstrap CI uses pooled AUROC over resampled items. So a card can claim “macro Δ has CI lower > 0” when the CI is actually for pooled Δ.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3713:Exact fix: bootstrap the same metric you report. Resample paired items within domain, recompute per-domain AUROC, then average domains. If a slice falls back to pooled AUROC, label it explicitly as pooled and use pooled for both point estimate and CI.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3715:Why it matters: the headline validity criterion, “bootstrap 95% CI lower bound > 0,” is currently mislabeled and can select false niches.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3761:The script tests roughly 21 slices per tool: 12 domains plus margin/tool-used/rank/turn slices. With CI lower > 0 from a two-sided 95% bootstrap, the positive-side null false-positive rate is about 2.5% per valid slice. Expected false positive niches are about `21 * 0.025 = 0.53` per tool, or `13 * 21 * 0.025 = 6.8` across 13 tools. Probability of at least one false positive across all tools is effectively near certain under independence; correlations reduce or reshape this, but do not solve snooping.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3801:1. Run a two-stage dev protocol: use dev-A to discover niches, dev-B to validate them with corrected macro-AUROC CIs, then freeze and run test once.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3832:`auroc_tool`, `auroc_direct`, and `delta` use macro AUROC when available, but the bootstrap CI uses pooled AUROC over resampled items. So a card can claim “macro Δ has CI lower > 0” when the CI is actually for pooled Δ.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3834:Exact fix: bootstrap the same metric you report. Resample paired items within domain, recompute per-domain AUROC, then average domains. If a slice falls back to pooled AUROC, label it explicitly as pooled and use pooled for both point estimate and CI.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3836:Why it matters: the headline validity criterion, “bootstrap 95% CI lower bound > 0,” is currently mislabeled and can select false niches.
refine-logs/CODEX_REVIEW_2026-04-18_v7.md:3882:The script tests roughly 21 slices per tool: 12 domains plus margin/tool-used/rank/turn slices. With CI lower > 0 from a two-sided 95% bootstrap, the positive-side null false-positive rate is about 2.5% per valid slice. Expected false positive niches are about `21 * 0.025 = 0.53` per tool, or `13 * 21 * 0.025 = 6.8` across 13 tools. Probability of at least one false positive across all tools is effectively near certain under independence; correlations reduce or reshape this, but do not solve snooping.
